WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.212
A practical Cyber Kill Chain routine starts with scope and purpose.

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:08.698
Decide whether the team is reviewing an incident, planning detections, evaluating controls,

00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:10.941
preparing an exercise, or summarizing intelligence.

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:15.801
Then collect evidence and place observed behavior into the stage that best fits.

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:18.044
Use careful language: observed, suspected, inferred,

00:00:18.044 --> 00:00:21.035
and unknown should not mean the same thing.

00:00:21.035 --> 00:00:25.521
Record confidence and assumptions so later readers understand how conclusions were reached.

00:00:25.521 --> 00:00:29.633
For each stage, ask what could detect, deny, disrupt, degrade, deceive,

00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:31.128
or contain the activity.

00:00:31.128 --> 00:00:35.240
Look for telemetry gaps, prevention gaps, response gaps, and recovery gaps.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.474
Translate those gaps into improvements with owners: a new log source, a refined alert,

00:00:40.474 --> 00:00:44.586
a hardened exposed service, a tested containment path, a clearer playbook,

00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:46.455
or a better executive summary.

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:49.446
The routine should produce decisions, not just labels.

00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:53.932
It should help defenders communicate what happened, what evidence supports the assessment,

00:00:53.932 --> 00:00:56.922
what risk remains, and what will be improved.

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:58.792
The core lesson is simple:

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:04.399
the Cyber Kill Chain is valuable when it turns intrusion progression into practical defensive action.

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:07.390
Use the routine after the incident as well.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.128
Revisit the mapped stages, compare expected evidence to actual evidence,

00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:15.240
and ask where the team depended on luck or manual effort.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.979
Those observations can become durable improvements to detections, controls, playbooks,

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:20.100
exercises, and reporting.
